  "content": {
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "hero",
        "data": {
          "title": "Content that answers",
          "body": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "h1a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "h1s1",
                  "text": "Field Station is a demo site managed in EmDash and optimized with Astro-AEO. Every page is editable in the admin, served on demand, and published with a markdown companion and an llms.txt index.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "faq": [],
          "sort": 1
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "features",
        "data": {
          "title": "What this site shows",
          "body": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "f1a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "f1s1",
                  "text": "Blog posts and portfolio case studies are published from a CMS, rendered on demand, and converted to markdown through the site's own routes. Drafts stay private, and the admin area stays out of the corpus.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "faq": [],
          "sort": 2
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "pricing",
        "data": {
          "title": "Pricing",
          "body": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "pr1a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "pr1s1",
                  "text": "This section is placeholder marketing content so the recipe can show a conversion-focused homepage built entirely from CMS entries.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "faq": [],
          "sort": 3
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "faq",
        "data": {
          "title": "Frequently asked questions",
          "body": [],
          "faq": [
            {
              "question": "Is the content editable?",
              "answer": "Yes. Posts, projects, and homepage sections are EmDash entries. Edit them in the admin at /_emdash/admin and publish; the next request serves the new revision."
            },
            {
              "question": "Do drafts appear in llms.txt?",
              "answer": "No. The page catalog lists published entries only, so drafts stay out of the corpus and their markdown companions are not served."
            },
            {
              "question": "How do markdown companions work here?",
              "answer": "Each page route renders on demand. A request for the .md companion rewrites into the page route, converts the HTML to markdown, and returns it with the page's own status."
            }
          ],
          "sort": 4
        }
      }
    ],
    "posts": [
      {
        "id": "post-signals",
        "slug": "signals-in-static-sites",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "Signals a static site sends to answer engines",
          "excerpt": "Plain, crawlable pages tell machines what a site knows. Here is how small signals add up.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p1a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p1s1",
                  "text": "A static site answers questions before it is asked to. Every page is a file, every file has one topic, and every topic is described in ordinary prose that a crawler can quote without running code.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p1b",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p1s2",
                  "text": "Three signals that matter",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p1c",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p1s3",
                  "text": "First, a markdown companion beside every page gives an exact representation of the content. Second, an llms.txt index at the root lists what exists and where. Third, structured data in JSON-LD names the entities on the page, so an answer engine can cite the source it actually used.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "taxonomies": {
          "category": [
            "engineering"
          ],
          "tag": [
            "static-sites",
            "llmstxt"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "post-portable",
        "slug": "portable-text-in-practice",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "Portable Text in practice",
          "excerpt": "Structured content is content you can render anywhere. Portable Text keeps meaning separate from markup.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p2a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p2s1",
                  "text": "Portable Text stores a document as structured blocks instead of an HTML string. The same source renders as a web page, a markdown companion, or an API response without parsing tags back out of a page.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p2b",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p2s2",
                  "text": "When a CMS keeps content structured, every consumer can pick the representation it needs. That is the whole argument: store meaning once, present it many ways.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "taxonomies": {
          "category": [
            "engineering"
          ],
          "tag": [
            "structured-content"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "post-crawling",
        "slug": "crawling-without-credentials",
        "status": "published",
        "data": {
          "title": "Crawling without credentials",
          "excerpt": "Aggregate corpora should render pages anonymously, the way a real crawler sees them.",
          "content": [
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p3a",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p3s1",
                  "text": "A corpus renderer that carries your session cookies produces a private index of a public site. Rendering pages anonymously, with credentials removed, keeps the published index honest and reproducible.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_type": "block",
              "_key": "p3b",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_type": "span",
                  "_key": "p3s2",
                  "text": "This post is published from EmDash, fetched through the site's own routes, and converted to markdown for the aggregate corpus. Editors change the text in the admin; the next corpus request reflects it.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "taxonomies": {
          "category": [
            "operations"
          ],
          "tag": [
            "llmstxt"
          ]
        }
      },
